Chapter 2 — Part 1
The transfer happened fast.
Nezu’s paperwork cleared the same week Cathleen left the hospital. One classified flight, one quiet meeting in the principal’s office, and Curtis Beardy was listed as an exchange student under Enhanced Physiology. No one outside a very short list knew about the Star and Stripe incident. No one knew about the morgue. Curtis arrived on the first day of the academic year with a single duffel, braids tied back, and the same low, unnamed warmth sitting behind his ribs that had been there since Johnny flatlined.
U.A. looked bigger in person. The gates were open. Students in the standard uniform moved in loose groups toward the main building. Curtis followed the map on his phone to Class 1-A. The door was already open. Inside, a green-haired kid stood near the front with a notebook half-open, talking to a stiff blue-haired boy in glasses and a round-faced girl with short brown hair. The rest of the room was filling in—loud blond in the back, pink skin, bird head, the usual spread of quirks on display even in civilian clothes.
Curtis stepped through the doorway. The conversation near the front slowed. A few heads turned. The girl with the brown hair—Uraraka, from the nameplate he could see—glanced over, then looked again. Her cheeks went a little pink. She shifted her weight. The low warmth in Curtis’s chest didn’t change. He filed the reaction the same way he filed every other one. New place. New people. Tall foreigner in the doorway. Nothing unusual.
“Exchange student,” he said. Accent flat. “Curtis Beardy. Enhanced Physiology.”
The blue-haired boy straightened immediately. “Tenya Iida. Welcome to Class 1-A. Please take your seat promptly so we may begin orientation on schedule.”
Curtis nodded once and moved to the empty desk near the back. The green-haired kid—Midoriya—watched him with wide eyes, muttering something under his breath about height and muscle density. Uraraka kept glancing over. Her fingers twisted in the hem of her skirt. She didn’t look away as fast as she probably meant to.
Aizawa arrived before anyone could settle.
The yellow sleeping bag unzipped in the doorway. Tired eyes, capture weapon loose around his neck, voice already done with the day. He didn’t waste time on introductions beyond the bare minimum. PE uniforms. Outside. Now. Orientation was canceled. The class filed out onto the grounds under a clear sky, the usual first-day nerves mixed with the sudden shift in plans.
Curtis changed with the rest of them. The PE uniform fit tight across the shoulders and chest. When he stepped back onto the field, a few of the girls looked longer than necessary. Yaoyorozu’s gaze tracked the line of his arms before she caught herself and turned toward the starting line. Jiro’s earjacks twitched. Uraraka stood a little closer to the group than she had inside, cheeks still warm. Curtis rolled his shoulders once and stayed quiet. Women looked. They always had. He was used to it.
Aizawa laid out the rules in the same flat tone he’d used in the classroom. Eight tests. Quirks allowed. Middle school scores were meaningless here. He used Bakugo for the demonstration pitch—the blond’s explosion cracked across the field and the ball vanished past the seven-hundred-meter mark. The class reacted. Excited. Competitive. Aizawa shut it down with one sentence.
“The student who places last will be expelled.”
Silence. Midoriya went pale. Iida’s hand shot up. Curtis stood with his arms loose at his sides and watched the teacher instead of the ball. The Divine Eye under his closed lid stayed quiet. Enhanced Physiology was the cover. He didn’t need to show anything else. Not on day one. Not with Nezu’s warning still fresh.
The tests started.
Fifty-meter dash. Iida’s engines lit. Uraraka lightened her clothes and cut her time. Curtis ran it straight—no flash, no visible quirk activation beyond the raw speed and power of a body that had spent years training past what should have been possible. He finished second behind Iida. Aizawa’s eyes narrowed a fraction. Curtis didn’t look at him.
Grip strength. Standing long jump. Side steps. He placed near the top in every physical measure without trying to stand out. The low warmth in his chest stayed constant. Uraraka kept ending up near him between events. Once she offered him a water bottle without being asked. Her fingers brushed his when he took it. She didn’t pull back right away. Her pulse jumped. Curtis thanked her, drank, and handed it back. He didn’t notice the way her thighs pressed together when she turned around.
By the time they reached the pitch, Midoriya was visibly struggling. He’d held back on the earlier tests. Now he stood at the line with the ball, arm shaking, clearly about to do something that would break him. Aizawa’s hair lifted. The erasure hit. Midoriya’s throw died at forty-six meters. The explanation followed—liability, self-injury, no rescue coming if you crippled yourself on purpose.
Curtis watched from the side. The Divine Eye mapped the moment Aizawa’s quirk cut the green-haired kid’s power. Mapped the way Midoriya’s shoulders set when he was given a second chance. Mapped the single finger that lit up on the next throw and sent the ball screaming past Bakugo’s mark. The class reacted. Bakugo exploded. Uraraka cheered. Curtis stayed where he was, arms crossed, expression even.
Aizawa’s gaze flicked to him once during the noise. Curtis met it. The teacher didn’t say anything. The last-place threat hung over the field until the scores posted and the ruse dropped. Midoriya wasn’t expelled. The class exhaled. Curtis rolled his neck, braids shifting against his back, and headed for the locker rooms with the rest of them.
Uraraka fell into step a few paces behind him. She didn’t speak. She just stayed close enough that the low, passive warmth in his chest registered her presence the same way it had registered Cathleen’s in the hospital room—constant, quiet, and completely unremarked.
He still didn’t know what it was.
He wouldn’t.
Aizawa’s voice cut across the field before Curtis reached the locker-room path.
“Beardy. You’re not finished.”
Curtis stopped. The rest of the class slowed with him. Midoriya was still flexing his broken finger. Bakugo had his hands in his pockets, scowling at the scoreboard. Uraraka stood a few paces back, water bottle forgotten in her hands. Aizawa stood at the pitch line with another softball, capture weapon loose, eyes half-lidded and sharp.
“I placed near the top on every test,” Curtis said. Accent flat. He didn’t raise his voice. “That’s the point of the ranking, ain’t it?”
“You placed near the top while holding back.” Aizawa tossed the ball once and caught it. “Enhanced Physiology doesn’t explain the gap between what you showed and what your file implies you can do. Throw.”
Curtis looked at the ball. Then at Aizawa. The Divine Eye stayed closed. He didn’t need it for this. The low warmth behind his ribs stayed quiet and constant while Uraraka shifted her weight behind him, close enough that he could hear her breath catch. He didn’t turn around.
“I already did the test.”
“And I’m telling you to do it again.” Aizawa’s tone didn’t change. “Unless you’d rather explain to Nezu why an exchange student refused a direct instruction on day one.”
Curtis exhaled through his nose. He walked back to the line. The class had gone quiet. Even Bakugo was watching. Curtis took the ball from Aizawa’s hand. It sat small in his palm. He rolled his shoulder once, set his feet, and didn’t bother with a wind-up that would have looked like everyone else’s.
He whipped it.
The motion was short and brutal—hips, shoulder, wrist, all of it snapping through in less than a second. The ball left his hand with a sharp, high whistle that cut across the field and kept going. It didn’t arc. It drilled. The sound dopplered as it climbed, then thinned into a distant crack when it finally punched through the far tree line and kept traveling. The measuring device on Aizawa’s tablet spiked, stuttered, and threw an error before it settled on a number past three thousand meters.
Silence.
Aizawa stared at the readout. His eyes flicked up to Curtis, then back to the number. The capture weapon didn’t move. Midoriya’s notebook was already open. Bakugo’s palms popped once, small and involuntary. Uraraka’s mouth was parted. Her cheeks had gone darker. She was standing closer to the line than she had been a minute ago, fingers tight around the water bottle, thighs pressed together under the PE shorts like she hadn’t noticed she was doing it.
Curtis rolled his wrist once and let his arm drop. The tape across his ribs pulled, but nothing tore. He looked at Aizawa.
“That enough?”
Aizawa didn’t answer right away. He locked the tablet, capture weapon shifting against his shoulders, and studied the tall exchange student like he was recalculating every assumption he’d walked onto the field with. The low, passive warmth in Curtis’s chest didn’t flare. It just stayed where it had been since the hospital—quiet, constant, and completely ignored while Uraraka took one small step closer and the rest of the class tried to process a throw that had left the measuring equipment behind.
“Get changed,” Aizawa said finally. His voice was the same. The look in his eyes wasn’t. “All of you. Beardy—my office after. We’re not done.”
Curtis nodded once. He turned toward the locker rooms. Uraraka fell into step a few paces behind him again without speaking. The whistle of the ball was still fading somewhere past the tree line. He didn’t look back at the scoreboard. He didn’t look at her.
He still didn’t know why she kept ending up near him.
He wouldn’t.
Aizawa’s eyes stayed on him. “Holding back on a test is one thing. Holding back when someone next to you needs the full output is another. I’ve watched students freeze because the first time they had to go past comfortable was the first time it mattered.”
Curtis’s jaw set. The accent stayed low and even, but the words came cleaner than the rough version that had been sitting in his chest.
“How many students in this class have actually killed something that was trying to kill them back?” He didn’t raise his voice. “There’s no harm in not breaking the measuring equipment on day one. If your worry is that I’ll lock up the second it gets real, you’re wrong.”
Aizawa didn’t interrupt. Curtis kept going, the same flat tone he used when he talked about trail markers or storm fronts.
“Appalachia ain’t just hills and coal. There are things in those mountains and tree lines that had power before anybody started calling it a quirk. Mothman. Deer women. Wendigos. Stuff that doesn’t show up on a registry because it was already old when the first quirk woke up. Faefolk that don’t care what you file them under. I grew up hearing the stories. Then I stopped hearing them and started running into them.” His good eye stayed level. “I didn’t make it out of those woods by cowering or by saving my strength for a scoreboard. I made it out because when something older than a hero license decided I was food, I used everything I had. Every time.”
The office was quiet. Aizawa’s capture weapon didn’t shift. He studied the tall exchange student the way he studied a structural report—looking for the load-bearing claims and the places that might crack under weight.
“Cryptids,” he said at last. Not mocking. Just naming it. “Nezu’s file doesn’t mention field experience against non-quirk entities.”
“Nezu’s file lists what he was allowed to list.” Curtis rolled one shoulder. The tape on his ribs pulled and settled. “I’m not asking you to put ‘fought a deer woman at fourteen’ on the training plan. I’m telling you the reason I didn’t throw the ball through the stratosphere on the first rep is the same reason I don’t light up every time something looks at me sideways. I’ve already been in the moments where holding back gets you dead. I know the difference.”
Aizawa was silent for a beat longer than usual. The tablet screen had gone dark. Outside, a distant door closed somewhere down the hall. When he spoke again, the tone hadn’t warmed, but the edge had shifted from suspicion to assessment.
“Noted. Doesn’t change the rule. You train at the level the exercise requires. If something on this campus turns out to be older than a quirk and decides the class is interesting, I expect the same output you used in those mountains. Not the version you showed the scoreboard.”
Curtis nodded once. “That’s fair.”
Aizawa jerked his chin at the door. “Dorms. Tomorrow we start seeing whether the rest of them can keep up with someone who already knows what freezing costs.”
Curtis turned and left. The low, passive warmth followed him into the hallway, still unnamed, still ignored. Behind him, Aizawa stayed at the desk a moment longer, eyes on the dark tablet, already recalculating what “Enhanced Physiology” was actually covering.
The rest of the class had already cleared the main path by the time Curtis stepped out of the building. Late afternoon light cut across the courtyard. Most of the noise had moved toward the dorms. He adjusted the strap of his duffel and started walking.
“Um—Beardy? Curtis?”
He stopped. Midoriya was a few paces behind him, notebook already half-open, pencil tucked behind one ear, the finger Recovery Girl had fixed still faintly pink. The green-haired kid looked like he’d been working up the nerve since the locker room and had finally run out of hallway to do it in.
Curtis turned. “Midoriya.”
“Izuku’s fine.” Midoriya caught up and fell into step instead of blocking the path, the way someone did when they didn’t want to make it a whole conversation but also weren’t ready to let it go. “That throw. Three thousand meters. Aizawa’s tablet errored out before it locked the number. I—I’ve never seen a physical enhancer do that without some kind of visible buildup. No smoke, no muscle swell, no kinetic tell. Just the whistle and then it was gone.”
Curtis kept walking. The low warmth in his chest stayed even. Midoriya wasn’t a threat and wasn’t staring the way some of the girls had. Just curious. Intense about it.
“Enhanced Physiology,” Curtis said. “I train. Always have.”
“Right, but—” Midoriya flipped a page without looking. His handwriting was already dense in the margins. “Most enhancers still show something when they push past baseline. Yours didn’t until Aizawa made you. And even then it was just… clean. Like you’d already done the math on how much force the ball could take before it came apart and stopped exactly there.” He glanced up. “Did you?”
Curtis’s mouth twitched. Not quite a smile. “Ball’s still in one piece somewhere past the tree line. Didn’t feel like testing the limit on school property.”
Midoriya nodded like that answered three questions and opened two more. They passed the edge of the main building. A couple of students from another class glanced over—mostly at Curtis’s height—then kept going. One of the girls slowed half a step, eyes tracking the line of his shoulders under the uniform jacket, then hurried to catch her friend. Curtis didn’t look. Midoriya did, briefly, then back at his notes.
“Aizawa called you in after,” Midoriya said. Careful. “He does that when he thinks someone’s sandbagging. He did it with me, kind of. Different reason.” His free hand flexed once, the repaired finger moving fine. “I’m not asking what you told him. Just… if you’re holding back because the quirk’s unstable, or because you don’t want the attention, or something else. People are already talking. Bakugo’s pissed. Uraraka asked me if I knew anything on the way to the dorms. I didn’t.”
Curtis stopped at the split in the path that led toward the 1-A dorm. The duffel strap creaked under his hand. He looked at Midoriya—really looked, the way he looked at trail signs and weather. The kid was vibrating with the need to understand, same way he’d vibrated on the field when Aizawa erased him and then gave him the second throw. Not malice. Just the kind of focus that got people hurt if they aimed it wrong.
“Quirk’s stable,” Curtis said. “I held back because day one ain’t the time to put a hole in the grounds and make everybody measure themselves against it. Aizawa wanted the number. He got it. Rest of the class saw what they needed to see to know I ain’t last place.” He shifted the duffel. “You broke a finger to stay in. That’s your business. Mine’s not making the first day about how far I can throw a ball when nobody’s dying.”
Midoriya’s pencil had stopped. He stared at the page for a second, then up. “You talk like you’ve already been in the part where people die.”
“Mountains don’t care if you’re enrolled.” Curtis’s accent flattened the words. “I’ll train at whatever level the exercise actually needs. Same as I told him. You writing that down?”
Midoriya blinked, then huffed a small, nervous laugh and closed the notebook. “Force of habit. Sorry.”
“Don’t be. Just don’t put anything in there that makes Aizawa think I’m a problem he has to solve in front of the class.” Curtis nodded toward the dorm path. “You headed that way?”
“Yeah.”
They walked the rest of the distance without talking much. Midoriya kept the notebook shut. Curtis kept his eyes on the building ahead. The low, passive warmth sat behind his ribs the whole way, quiet and constant, registering the occasional glance from students they passed and filing every one of them under the same heading it always had.
New place. Tall transfer. Nothing else to see.
They didn’t turn toward the dorms.
Midoriya hesitated at the split, then nodded when Curtis kept walking the long way around the outer path. “Train station’s fine. I was gonna head home anyway. Mom’s expecting me. Dorm move-in’s not until next week for most of us.”
Curtis adjusted the duffel. The low warmth behind his ribs stayed level while they crossed the open stretch of campus toward the gates. A few students still lingered near the main building. One girl from General Studies slowed when they passed, eyes catching on the height and the braids, then looked away fast when Midoriya glanced over. Curtis didn’t.
They cleared the gates and started down the sidewalk toward the station. Traffic was light. Midoriya had the notebook under his arm instead of open.
“You said the quirk’s stable,” he started, careful. “But Aizawa was right that the throw didn’t read like a normal enhancer. Most of those still have a tell when they push. Yours didn’t. And you talk about the mountains like the power was already there before anybody tested you.”
Curtis watched the crosswalk light change. “Because it was.”
Midoriya looked up.
“Enhanced Physiology’s what they wrote down so the paperwork would clear,” Curtis said. Accent low, even. “Ain’t wrong. Just ain’t the whole of it. Where I’m from, old folks still use the older words for it. Superhuman. Not a quirk that woke up one day. A body that came out the way it is. Stronger frame. Denser muscle. Faster recovery. Reflexes that don’t need a trigger. I spent years training it because I thought I was quirkless and that was all I had. Turns out the training just made what was already there harder to hide.”
He stepped off the curb when the light changed. Midoriya matched him.
“You don’t activate it,” Midoriya said slowly. “It’s just… on.”
“Baseline.” Curtis rolled one shoulder. The duffel shifted. “I can push past it. Same as anybody can push past what they’re used to. Difference is the floor’s higher. Aizawa saw the floor on the first seven tests and the ceiling on the eighth. That’s why he called it holding back.”
They reached the station stairs. Midoriya stopped on the landing, notebook still closed, processing. Curtis kept going a few steps, then paused when the kid didn’t follow.
“There’s other shit that might not connect,” he added. “Blood type’s Rh null. Rare. Doctors back home used to joke I was walking around with the kind they put in special freezers. Vision’s 20/1. Not a quirk thing either. Just how the eyes came. I can read a trail sign at a distance most people need binoculars for. Don’t know if any of it ties to the rest. Never had a reason to ask until people started writing ‘Enhanced Physiology’ on forms and expecting it to explain a softball going through a tree line.”
Midoriya’s fingers tightened on the notebook. He didn’t open it. “Rh null and 20/1. And a body that was already past normal before you ever trained it.” He exhaled. “That’s not in any of the public registries I’ve read. Nezu cleared you anyway.”
“Nezu clears what he decides is worth the trouble.” Curtis glanced toward the platform clock. “Train’s in eight minutes. You writing any of that down later?”
“Probably.” Midoriya’s mouth twitched, nervous but honest. “Not the parts that sound like they belong in a classified file. Just… the functional stuff. So I don’t assume wrong if we’re paired on something.”
“Fair.”
They headed down to the platform. The low, passive warmth stayed quiet in Curtis’s chest the whole way, registering the station noise and the occasional glance and filing all of it under the same heading it always had. Midoriya stood a careful distance away, notebook under his arm, already turning the new information over like another page he hadn’t earned the right to fill yet.
Curtis watched the rails and didn’t offer anything else.
He still hadn’t mentioned the eyes.
The platform clock ticked down. Midoriya still hadn’t opened the notebook. He stood with it tucked under his arm like he was waiting for permission he hadn’t asked for, the way he did when he wanted the data and didn’t want to take it without the other person knowing.
Curtis watched him for a second, then the corner of his mouth lifted. Not wide. Just enough to take the flat edge off his face.
“Go on,” he said. “Write it in the profile. I saw you scribbling notes on everybody during class. Bakugo’s output. Iida’s acceleration. Uraraka’s timing on the dash. You weren’t hiding it.” The accent stayed low. “Being well studied is how you get to the top. Heroes who only know their own power lose to the ones who already mapped the room. You figured that out before most of them did.”
Midoriya’s ears went pink. His fingers tightened on the notebook, then loosened. For a second he looked like he might argue—old habit, the part of him that still expected people to call the notes weird or creepy. Curtis just stood there, duffel strap over one shoulder, expression easy, like he’d handed over a trail map and expected it to get used.
“I—yeah.” Midoriya exhaled, small and nervous, and finally flipped the notebook open. The page for Class 1-A already had tight columns. He found the blank line under the exchange student and started writing, pencil moving fast. “Enhanced Physiology—baseline superhuman physiology, not a standard activation quirk. Higher floor on strength, speed, recovery. Rh null blood type. 20/1 vision. Trained extensively pre-U.A. Prefers controlled output unless required…” He glanced up once, checking. “That last part okay?”
“Accurate.”
Midoriya nodded and kept going, muttering under his breath the way he did when the analysis locked in—something about force transfer on the throw, lack of visible kinetic buildup, possible connective tissue density. The train brakes hissed down the platform. He didn’t look up until the doors opened.
“Thanks,” he said, quieter. “Most people tell me to put the notebook away.”
“Most people ain’t trying to stay ahead of whatever’s coming.” Curtis shifted the duffel. “You got the notes. Use ’em. Just don’t put anything in there that’ll get you pulled into a classified meeting if Aizawa decides to read over your shoulder.”
Midoriya huffed a short laugh and closed the book, tucking it safe under his arm again. “Deal.”
The train doors stayed open. Midoriya stepped on. Curtis stayed on the platform, one hand lifted in a short nod as the kid turned back.
“See you tomorrow, Beardy.”
“Curtis is fine.”
The doors slid shut. Midoriya’s green hair disappeared behind the glass as the train pulled out. Curtis watched it go, then turned toward the long walk back to the dorms that still weren’t officially his yet. The low, passive warmth in his chest stayed exactly where it had been all day—quiet, constant, unnamed.
He still didn’t know what it was.
The train had already cleared the platform when the voice hit.
“Hello there, young Beardy!”
Curtis turned. All Might stood at the top of the station stairs in full muscle form—blonde hair spiked, costume tight across the chest, smile wide enough to catch the late light. A couple of commuters did double-takes and kept walking. One kid with a phone fumbled it and nearly dropped it. All Might didn’t seem to notice. He came down the steps two at a time, cape settling, and stopped a respectful distance away like he’d practiced not crowding people who weren’t asking for autographs.
Curtis shifted the duffel on his shoulder. The low warmth in his chest didn’t spike. All Might wasn’t a woman and wasn’t staring the way some of the students had. Just loud.
“Hello there,” Curtis said. Accent even. “You’re All Might. Cathleen told me a lot about you.”
All Might’s smile shifted—still bright, but the edges softened into something more personal. “Star and Stripe! Yes, she mentioned she’d spoken with you before the transfer. Said you were the exchange student Nezu cleared and that I should introduce myself properly instead of letting the first impression be a television screen.” He laughed, the big one. “She also said you don’t rattle easy. I can see she wasn’t exaggerating.”
Curtis nodded once. “She talks straight. Figured you would too.”
“I try.” All Might glanced toward the empty tracks, then back. The heroic posture stayed, but his voice dropped half a notch, the way it did when he was talking to someone instead of performing for a crowd. “Aizawa already pulled you in after the test. I saw the throw from the observation deck. Three thousand meters and change. Clean. No wasted motion.” He tilted his head. “Cathleen didn’t give me details. Just that you’d already been through something that made the usual first-day nerves irrelevant. I’m not here to pry. I’m here because you’re in my class starting tomorrow, and I’d rather the Symbol of Peace say hello on day one than wait for a crisis.”
Curtis’s mouth twitched. Almost a smile. “Appreciate it. Ain’t much for ceremonies either.”
“Good. Aizawa hates them.” All Might’s grin came back full force. “If you need anything—training times, recovery windows, someone to translate Aizawa’s particular brand of encouragement—my door’s open. Cathleen vouched for you. That carries weight with me.”
“I’ll keep it in mind.”
All Might gave him a firm nod, the kind that felt like a handshake without the hands, then turned to head back up the stairs. A small cluster of fans had gathered at the landing. He waved once, big and easy, and the group lit up. Curtis watched him go, duffel strap creaking under his grip.
The low, passive warmth stayed quiet behind his ribs the whole time. Unnamed. Constant. Already folding the new conversation into the rest of the day like it belonged there.
Curtis adjusted the duffel and glanced toward the tree line beyond the station road. “Can you come with me somewhere?”
All Might’s eyebrows lifted. The big smile stayed, but the laugh that followed was shorter, curious. “Lead the way, young man. I have time before the evening patrol window.”
They walked. Curtis didn’t explain. All Might didn’t push. The path curved off the main road, past a maintenance gate that wasn’t locked, and into a stretch of woods the city hadn’t finished developing. Ten minutes later the trees opened into a wide, empty clearing—packed dirt, old tire tracks, no cameras, no foot traffic. Late light cut through the canopy in clean bars.
Curtis set the duffel down at the edge and rolled his shoulders. The tape on his ribs pulled and settled. He turned to face All Might, expression even.
“Forgive me,” he said. Accent low. “There’s two people in Japan I want to clash strength with. You. And my favorite hero—the one I actually look up to. The Rabbit Hero: Mirko.”
All Might’s smile didn’t drop, but it changed. The performance edge eased. He studied the tall exchange student the way he studied a new variable on a battlefield—measuring reach, stance, the quiet way Curtis had planted his feet like this wasn’t a request he expected to be refused.
“Mirko,” All Might repeated. Respect in it. “She’s particular about who she spars. Doesn’t waste time on people who only want the story afterward.” He tilted his head. “And you want to start with me.”
“Figured I’d ask the one who’s already here.” Curtis’s good eye stayed level. “Cathleen said you still answer when someone needs a straight measurement instead of a speech. I ain’t looking for a lesson. Just want to know where the floor is against someone who doesn’t have to hold the Symbol face on.”
All Might was quiet for a beat. Then the laugh came back—lower this time, genuine. He reached up and loosened the cape clasp, folding the fabric over one arm so it wouldn’t tangle. The muscle form stayed. The posture shifted into something more ready.
“Very well, young Beardy.” He set his feet. “One exchange. No quirks beyond what you already carry. You tell me when you’ve felt what you came for. And if Mirko ever agrees to the second half of that list, I expect a full report. She hits harder than the highlight reels show.”
Curtis nodded once. The low, passive warmth in his chest stayed quiet and constant, irrelevant here. He rolled his neck, braids shifting, and raised his hands.
“Appreciate it.”
All Might’s grin sharpened. “Then come.”
The clearing stayed empty. The light kept falling. Neither of them had thrown anything yet.
They moved at the same time.
All Might closed the distance in a blur of controlled power. Curtis met him. No wind-up. No quirk flash. Just the full baseline of a body that had been superhuman before anyone wrote it down as one. Their foreheads cracked together in the center of the clearing.
The impact detonated.
Dirt erupted outward in a perfect ring. The shockwave ripped leaves off the nearest trees and slammed into the packed ground hard enough to buckle it. A crater opened under their feet—jagged, smoking at the edges, deep enough that the tire tracks vanished. The sound rolled through the woods and kept going.
All Might felt it in his teeth.
Too clean, he thought, boots dug in, neck braced against the force still traveling up his spine. No wasted kinetic bleed. No enhancer flare. He met me at the point of contact and didn’t fold. Cathleen wasn’t exaggerating. This isn’t a student testing a ceiling. This is someone who already knows what his floor costs and decided the Symbol of Peace was the right measuring stick.
The pressure broke.
Curtis left the ground. He flew backward, boots carving twin furrows through the dirt before he hit and rolled, shoulder first, braids whipping. He came up on one knee at the edge of the crater, chest heaving, tape on his ribs strained but holding. Dirt streaked his uniform. Blood welled at his hairline where the clash had split skin.
He was laughing.
Not the wild kind. Low, rough, genuine. He wiped the blood with the back of his wrist and looked across the broken ground at All Might, good eye bright.
“Amazing,” he said. Accent thick with it. “Absolutely amazing. You truly are number one.”
All Might straightened slowly. The crater smoked between them. His own head rang, but the muscle form held. He watched the exchange student kneel there grinning like the impact had been a gift instead of a warning, and the thought settled heavier than the shockwave.
He laughed. Most don’t. Most measure themselves, flinch, and recalculate. He just wanted the truth of it. All Might’s smile stayed, smaller now. Nezu was right to bring him. And Mirko is going to have opinions.
Curtis pushed to his feet, still smiling, and rolled his neck until it popped. The low, passive warmth in his chest hadn’t moved. It didn’t need to. This wasn’t about that.
“Again?” he asked.
All Might’s cape stirred in the settling dust. “Once more. Then we both walk out of here under our own power. Deal?”
“Deal.”
The second clash ended cleaner than the first.
Curtis hit the dirt on his back at the far edge of the new crater, air leaving him in one hard grunt. He stayed down. One arm flung out, the other resting across the tape on his ribs. Braids fanned in the dust. He was still smiling, smaller now, chest rising and falling while he stared up at the canopy.
All Might stood over the broken ground with his arms crossed, cape stirred by the last of the shockwave. The muscle form hadn’t dropped. Dirt streaked one shoulder. He looked impressed in the quiet way he got when something rewrote a calculation he’d already made—shoulders loose, mouth curved, eyes sharp.
Not an enhancer, he thought, watching the way Curtis’s breathing evened out instead of hitching into pain. Or not only one. The output is too even. No spiral. No burnout lag. He met me twice and the only thing that changed was how deep the crater got. Cathleen said the file was thin on purpose. Nezu doesn’t thin files unless the thing inside them doesn’t fit the columns.
Curtis turned his head. Good eye found All Might through the settling dust.
All Might didn’t ask out loud. The question sat in the line of his posture anyway.
Curtis didn’t answer it. He pushed up onto his elbows, ribs pulling, and wiped blood from his brow with the side of his wrist. The smile stayed.
“Cathleen tell you?” he asked. Accent rough from the impacts. “Or did she leave that part out too?”
All Might’s arms stayed crossed. The smile didn’t leave, but it shifted into something more careful. “She told me you were worth the transfer. She told me you’d already survived something that made first-day posturing irrelevant. She did not tell me what, precisely, Nezu classified.” He tilted his head. “I’m not asking you to break that. I’m asking whether the thing I just measured is the thing the paperwork claims, or whether I need to plan around a variable the rest of the staff hasn’t been cleared for.”
Curtis let his head drop back against the dirt for a second. The low, passive warmth in his chest sat exactly where it always did—quiet, constant, irrelevant to the crater and the man standing in it. He laughed once, short.
“She told you what she was allowed to tell you,” he said. “Same as Nezu. Same as the form.” He sat up fully, forearms on his knees, and looked at All Might across the broken ground. “You felt the floor. That’s the part that matters for training. Rest of it stays where they put it until somebody higher than both of us decides otherwise.”
All Might studied him another moment. Then he nodded, once, the way he did when he accepted a boundary he didn’t love but wouldn’t cross.
“Fair enough, young Beardy.” He uncrossed his arms and offered a hand. “On your feet. We’re done measuring for today. And if Mirko ever takes that second request, tell her I already collected the first data point. She’ll want the comparison.”
Curtis took the hand. All Might pulled him up without effort. The crater smoked between them. Neither of them filled the silence with the question that was still sitting there.
They left the crater behind them. Dust still hung in the clearing, but the path back toward the station road was quiet. All Might walked with the cape folded over one arm again, muscle form steady, pace matched to Curtis’s instead of the usual ground-eating stride he used on patrol. The duffel bumped against Curtis’s hip. His ribs ached in a clean way. He didn’t favor them.
All Might glanced over after a minute. The question came easy, no performance in it.
“Why Mirko?”
Curtis watched the tree line. “Saw her on the feeds back home. Not the big sponsored spots. The raw ones. Alley clearances. Highway stops. The kind where the camera’s already late and she’s already finished.” His accent stayed low. “She never went to U.A. Doesn’t run a company. No sidekicks on payroll, no agency letterhead. Independent. Takes the contracts she wants, turns down the ones that want her face more than her work.”
All Might nodded, listening. He’d heard the same details from pro networks. Hearing them from an eighteen-year-old who’d just matched him twice in a dirt clearing made them land different.
“Most heroes your age chase the structure,” All Might said. “School. Agency. Ranking. She skipped all of it and still sits near the top.”
“That’s why.” Curtis rolled one shoulder. The tape pulled. “Back in the mountains, nobody handed you a license and a team when something came out of the tree line. You handled it or you didn’t. She built her name the same way. No pipeline. No safety net with a logo on it. Just the work and whatever she decided the work was worth.” He glanced at All Might. “I respect that. Cathleen’s strong. You’re the Symbol. Mirko’s the one who proved you can stay at the top without asking the system to carry any of the weight.”
All Might was quiet for a few steps. The station lights were visible ahead through the branches. When he spoke, the smile was smaller, real.
“She’ll like hearing that more than another ranking speech. If you ever get the spar, lead with it. She has even less patience for flattery than Aizawa.”
“Wasn’t planning on flattery.”
“Good.” All Might’s cape stirred. “Then you’ll probably survive the first exchange. Maybe.”
Curtis huffed a short laugh. They kept walking. The low, passive warmth in his chest stayed exactly where it had been since the hospital—quiet, constant, and still unnamed while the Symbol of Peace matched his pace all the way back to the road.
All Might’s smile widened. He leaned in just enough that the height difference didn’t swallow the look, cape still folded over his arm, voice dropping into the conspiratorial register he used when he was enjoying himself.
“What else?”
Curtis’s ears went warm. The heat climbed fast across his cheekbones and stayed there, visible even in the low station light. He kept his eyes on the path ahead, jaw set, accent flatter than usual.
“Nothing at all.”
All Might laughed—the big one, bright and unbothered, echoing off the trees for a second before it settled. He straightened, still grinning, and gave Curtis’s shoulder a light clap that didn’t quite rock him.
“Noted, young Beardy. Noted.” The smile didn’t fade. “Mirko does have that effect on people who’ve actually watched the work instead of the posters. You’re in good company.”
Curtis didn’t answer. The blush held. He adjusted the duffel strap and kept walking, boots steady on the packed dirt while All Might matched his pace, still quietly amused, all the way back toward the lights of the station road.
The low, passive warmth in Curtis’s chest stayed exactly where it always was. Quiet. Constant. Irrelevant to the heat still sitting on his face.
